Consider a rocket that ejects fuel with speed $u$ relative to the rocket, moving in a straight line. The process of ejection can be complicated, but the total momentum must obey $dot(P) = F^"ext"$, so comparing momenta at times $t$ and $t + delta t$ gives
$
  m dot(v) + u dot(m) = F^"ext",
$
called the Tsiolkovsky rocket equation. Equivalently,
$
  m dot(v) = F^"ext" - u dot(m),
$
where $-u dot(m)$ is the thrust force exerted on the rocket by the ejected fuel.
